<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Mira</h1><p class="st-meta">1320 BCE – 1180 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Mira, the strongest of the vassal kingdoms carved out of Arzawa, whose king's name was read on the Karabel rock relief near Izmir.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mursili-ii">Mursili II</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> defeated <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/arzawa">Arzawa</a> c. 1320 BCE, he made Mashuiluwa, a prince who had sought refuge at the Hittite court, king of Mira, the land around the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyuk-menderes-river">Maeander</a> valley and its neighbour Kuwaliya.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Mira's kings ruled by treaty with the Hittite great king, and their line included Kupanta-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurunta">Kurunta</a>. A reading of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabel-relief">Karabel relief</a> near <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/smyrna">Izmir</a>, published in 1998, names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarkasnawa">Tarkasnawa</a>, king of Mira, showing his realm reached towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>. Its people spoke Luwian.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Mashuiluwa rebelled and was deposed, but Mira stayed a Hittite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> until the empire collapsed c. 1180 BCE. The western Anatolian kingdoms then disappear from records; the region later became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mira.jpg" alt="Mira" width="320" height="235"><figcaption>Image: Digitized Sky Survey 2. Acknowledgment: Davide De Martin. · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Wide-field_view_of_the_sky_around_a_field_studied_in_the_MASSIV_survey_(eso1212d)(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1320 BCE – 1180 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mashuiluwa, Kupanta-Kurunta, Tarkasnawa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Luwian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Luwian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Turkey</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mira-Kuwaliya</dd></dl></aside></div>
